<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raymond VII, Count of Toulouse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1197–<a href="/years/1249">1249</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raymond VII was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> from 1222 to 1249 and the last of his line. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> he made peace with the French crown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1229">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1229, ceding the Comtat Venaissin to the Church and marrying his heiress Joan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-count-of-poitiers">Alphonse of Poitiers</a>. On their deaths in 1271 the county passed to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raymond-vii-count-of-toulouse.jpg" alt="Raymond VII, Count of Toulouse" width="276" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raymond7toulouse.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raymond was born in July 1197 at the Château de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaucaire">Beaucaire</a>, the son of Raymond VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and Joan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Through his mother he was a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> and a nephew of the kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> and John. In March 1211, at the age of 13, he married Sancha of Aragon; they had one daughter, Joan, and were divorced in 1241.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a>, in May 1216, Raymond set out from Marseille and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaucaire">Beaucaire</a>, which he captured on 24 August. He fought to reconquer the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> from Simon de Montfort and later from Simon's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amaury-vi-of-montfort">Amaury VI of Montfort</a>. He succeeded his father in 1222 as count of Toulouse, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> and marquis of Provence, and at his accession he and Roger Bernard II the Great, the new count of Foix, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a>.</p><p>In 1225 the council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourges">Bourges</a> excommunicated him and launched a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against him. King Louis VIII wished to renew the conflict in order to enforce his royal rights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a>, and rejected an embassy from Roger Bernard, so the counts of Foix and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> took up arms again. The war was largely a discontinuous series of skirmishes, and in January 1229 Raymond, defeated, was forced to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1229">Treaty of Paris</a>, also known as the Treaty of Meaux. By it his daughter Joan was made to marry Alphonse, the brother of the king.</p><p>In 1242 Raymond allied with Count Hugh of La Marche and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a> against Louis IX in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintonge-war">Saintonge War</a>. Louis sent an army under the constable Imbert de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaujeu">Beaujeu</a> and Bishop Hugh of Clermont, and Raymond was forced to surrender the castles of Saverdun and Bram. In 1243 he married Margaret of Lusignan, who bore no children, and the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyons</a> granted him a divorce in 1245. He then sought to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatrice-of-provence">Beatrice of Provence</a>, but she married Charles, a son of Blanche, the mother of Louis IX.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Raymond died on 27 September 1249 and was buried beside his mother Joan in Fontevrault Abbey. Alphonse became count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, and after Alphonse's death the county was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raymond VII of Toulouse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Raymond VI and his son, the future <b>Raymond VII</b>, fought back, retaking Beaucaire in 1216 and Toulouse in 1217, and Simon’s death in 1218 seemed to reverse the conquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Paris of 1229 after the Albigensian Crusade, <b>Raymond VII</b> ceded it to the Church, but the French crown held it until Philip III handed it to Pope Gregory X in 1274.</blockquote><a href="/state/comtat-venaissin">Comtat Venaissin</a></li><li><blockquote>It joined the opposition to the counts of Provence and backed <b>Raymond VII</b> of Toulouse.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-marseille">Republic of Marseille</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Beaucaire, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Creissels, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.81,4.64&amp;year=1230" aria-label="Beaucaire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Raymond VII, Count of Toulouse"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5286 3825h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5286 3825h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.81,4.64&amp;year=1230">What was here in 1230? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
